<div style="text-align:center; font-size:28px; font-weight:bold;">
    Applying Functions to DataFrames
</div>
<br>
<div style="font-size:18px;">

**Important Note:** When applying a function that needs to access multiple columns, use `axis=1` in the `.apply()` function. This tells pandas to pass entire rows as series objects to your function instead of just single column values. You can access column values within the function using `row['column_name']` for example.

```python
# If your function needs just one column:
df['new_col'] = df['column'].apply(your_function)

# If your function needs multiple columns:
df['new_col'] = df.apply(your_function, axis=1)  # function receives each row as a Series

# To access columns within your function:
def your_function(row):
    value1 = row['first_column']  
    value2 = row['second_column']
    return # some calculation with column values 
```
 
